<?xml version="1.0"?>
<rdf:RDF xmlns="https://w3id.org/aias/odp/iso22989#"
     xml:base="https://w3id.org/aias/odp/iso22989"
     xmlns:owl="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#"
     xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#"
     xmlns:xml="http://www.w3.org/XML/1998/namespace"
     xmlns:xsd="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#"
     xmlns:bibo="http://purl.org/ontology/bibo/"
     xmlns:rdfs="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#"
     xmlns:skos="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#"
     xmlns:vann="http://purl.org/vocab/vann/"
     xmlns:terms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/">
    <owl:Ontology rdf:about="https://w3id.org/aias/odp/iso22989">
        <owl:versionIRI rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/aias/odp/iso22989/2.0.0"/>
        <terms:bibliographicCitation>TODO: citation of the accompanying publication</terms:bibliographicCitation>
        <terms:contributor>TODO: co-authors</terms:contributor>
        <terms:created rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2026-08-19</terms:created>
        <terms:creator>TODO: author name, ORCID</terms:creator>
        <terms:description xml:lang="en">Ontology design pattern for the terminology of artificial intelligence according to ISO/IEC 22989:2022, Information technology, Artificial intelligence, Artificial intelligence concepts and terminology.

The pattern describes an AI system in three views: what it is made of and what it does, which algorithms and models carry it, and which data it runs on. The standard supplies an internationally agreed vocabulary for all three.

One distinction runs through the whole pattern. Symbolic AI, 3.1.33, and subsymbolic AI, 3.1.34, both rest on models, but only the second learns them. The pattern therefore keeps the general model, 3.1.23, above the machine learning model, 3.3.7, so that a rule-based system can be recorded without pretending it was trained.

The pattern is one of four subdomain patterns of the AIAS information model. It is designed to be imported through owl:imports and aligned by way of the AIAS alignment ontology. Named individuals for the qualities are supplied separately in ISO22989-instances.ttl.</terms:description>
        <terms:license rdf:resource="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/"/>
        <terms:publisher>TODO: institution</terms:publisher>
        <terms:rights>TODO: funding statement, projects EKI and OptiFlex, dtec.bw</terms:rights>
        <terms:source rdf:resource="https://www.iso.org/standard/74296.html"/>
        <terms:title xml:lang="en">ISO/IEC 22989 Ontology Design Pattern</terms:title>
        <bibo:status xml:lang="en">Draft</bibo:status>
        <vann:preferredNamespacePrefix>iso22989</vann:preferredNamespacePrefix>
        <vann:preferredNamespaceUri>https://w3id.org/aias/odp/iso22989#</vann:preferredNamespaceUri>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Normative basis: ISO/IEC 22989:2022.

Clause 3 of the standard holds 115 entries and this pattern takes up 47 of them. REFERENCE.md lists every entry with the reason it is taken or left out, so the selection is checkable rather than asserted. Three whole clauses stay out: neural networks, natural language processing and computer vision name techniques and application fields, both open sets. A model using a convolutional network records it as a named individual of the model algorithm class instead.

Some classes here have no entry in clause 3. Five of them are collecting classes the standard needs but does not name, such as the common superclass of the four kinds of learning. The rest are constructs of this work. Every one of them says so on its own skos:note, and REFERENCE.md lists them apart from the entries.

No class of another namespace appears in this pattern. Where a term matches a term of another standard, as control, 3.5.5, matches IEC 60050-351, 351-42-19, word for word, the two are related in the alignment ontology rather than here. A pattern that referenced another would drag it along wherever it is used.</rdfs:comment>
        <owl:versionInfo>2.0.0</owl:versionInfo>
    </owl:Ontology>
    


